  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings rely on self-reported walking frequencies and environmental perceptions, which may introduce recall bias, and the sample is limited to middle-aged adults in two Dutch cities.",
      "what": "This study analyzed survey data from 366 adults aged 45 to 65 in Rotterdam and Maastricht, the Netherlands, to examine how BMI, self-perceived health, and environmental features relate to non-commute walking frequency. Using zero-inflated Poisson regression models, the researchers measured objective environmental characteristics within 300 m, 600 m, and 1000 m buffers of homes alongside self-reported environmental perceptions. The analysis revealed that BMI, street connectivity, and perceived aesthetics were negatively associated with walking frequency, while address density and perceived safety showed positive associations.",
      "why": "The study demonstrates that objective and perceived environmental features contribute equally to explaining non-commute walking behaviors, and that these environmental factors influence walking independently of an individual's health status rather than being moderated by it."
    }
  },
